## Prerequisites

```bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh
rustup target add {% rust_target %}
cargo install --locked stellar-cli@{% stellar_cli_version %}
```

## Test

```bash
cargo test
```

The tests use `env.mock_auths(...)` rather than `env.mock_all_auths()` for the
access-control cases. That distinction matters: `mock_all_auths` makes every
`require_auth` succeed, so a test using it would still pass if you deleted the
guard. The selective form authorizes one address for one call, which is what
proves a stranger is actually turned away.

## Build

```bash
{% build_command %}
```

Produces `target/{% rust_target %}/release/{% crate_name %}.wasm`. Use this
rather than `cargo build`: the Stellar CLI selects the correct target and
attaches the contract metadata the network expects.

## Deploy

The constructor argument is supplied at deploy time:

```bash
stellar keys generate --global alice --network testnet --fund

stellar contract deploy \
  --wasm target/{% rust_target %}/release/{% crate_name %}.wasm \
  --source alice \
  --network testnet \
  -- --admin "$(stellar keys address alice)"
```

## Where to take it next

- Move from one admin to a role map (`DataKey::Role(Address)`) if you need more
  than a single privilege level.
- Make `transfer_admin` two-step: store a pending admin, and add an `accept_admin`
  that the pending address must call.
- Emit events on privileged changes so off-chain systems can follow them.
